{"version":2,"text":"Snakebite in children: Texts\n\nText A\n\nBackground\n\nSnakebite is uncommon in Victoria and envenomation (systemic poisoning from the bite) is rare. The bite site\nmay be evidenced by fang marks, one or multiple scratches. The bite site may be painful, swollen or bruised,\nbut usually is not for snakes in Victoria.\n\nThere are no sea snakes in Victoria, but land-based snakes can swim.\n\nMajor venomous snakes in Victoria and effects of envenomation:\n\nSnake\n\nCoagulopathy\n\nNeurotoxicity\n\nMyotoxicity\n\nBrown\n\nVICC\n\nRare and mild\n\n-\n\nTiger\n\nVICC\n\n30%\n\n20%\n\nRed-\nMild increase\nbellied\nText C in aPITT and\nblack\nINR with\nnormal\nfibrinogen,\nusually no\nsignificant\nbleeding\n\n-\n\nUncommon\n\nSystematic\nsymptoms\n\nCardiovascular\neffects\n\nTMA\n\n50%\n\nCollapse (35%)\nCardiac arrest (5%)\n\n10%\n\nCommon\n\nRare\n\n5%\n\nCommon\nOften\nsignificant\nbite site pain\nand limb\nswelling\n\n-\n\n-\n\nVICC: Venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy (abnormal INR, high aPTT, fibrinogen very low, D-dimer\nhigh).\n\nMyotoxicity muscle pain, tenderness, rhabdomyolysis\n\nSystemic Symptoms see history and examination.\n\nTMA: thrombotic microangiography. Haemolysis with fragmented red blood cells on blood film,\nthrombocytopenia and a rising creatinine.\n\nPage 368","layout":{"version":1,"width":595.276001,"height":841.890015,"blocks":[{"text":"Snakebite in children: Texts","lines":[{"text":"Snakebite in children: Texts","x":0.37635516907055694,"y":0.04731688734899653,"width":0.2269231411531405,"height":0.01713287928708835,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Snakebite","x":0.37635516907055694,"width":0.08323735530537543,"confidence":1},{"text":"in","x":0.4638661722228577,"width":0.015905227128415713,"confidence":1},{"text":"children:","x":0.4840450471981988,"width":0.07290853306212823,"confidence":1},{"text":"Texts","x":0.5604007207406301,"width":0.04287758948306737,"confidence":1}]}]},{"text":"Text A","lines":[{"text":"Text A","x